V. Rfc-SULTS OF THE IxQUIKY.

The chief points of interest which the exhil)ition of these scales affords appear ¶ be the following:

1. The predominance of the Fourth, and mere evolution of the Fifth, in Greece, ¶ India, and Japan.

These maj- be only different forms of some ¶ system. The Chinese may have ¶ the principle, but on this the ex¶ uncertainty pervading all exhibitions of ¶ scales hitherto made (including Van ¶ treatise on Chinese Music, 1884), renders ¶ difficult to judge. The Fourths actually ¶ are uncertain, see Nos. 103 to 109. But ¶ seem at home in Japan, where they are ¶ used iu tuning, but may have been imported, ¶ they are occasionally absent. In the im¶ Javese scales, Nos. 94 to 102, they are ¶ in tune. Even in Arabia aud India they ¶ apt to be altered. The specimens of ruder

music, Nos. 81 to 87, are not favourable to the Fourth. The Fifth never had the same predominance. It is constantly too sharp or too flat. In modern India generally it is too flat. In one set of scales in Java it is too sharp (No. 94); in the other set as flat, as in India (Nos. 74 to 79). These differences probably pervaded also the scales of other countries as actually used, but we know Greece and Arabia from theory only. That the Fifth is true on the bagpipe (No. 52) depends apparently on the use of the drone, which would produce frightful beats if it were as much out of tune as in the other cases cited.

2. The use of Tones and Semitones of about 200 and 100 cents depends upon ¶ (ireek tetrachordal system as modified by Pythagorean intonation.

In Zalzal's scale (No. 51) and even in the ¶ Arabic scales, Nos. 57 to 65 (that is, ¶ the three Nos. 54 to 56, which are ¶ with the Greek, and the exceptional ¶ Nos. 68 to 72), they do not both exist. ¶ the Indian scales they are overridden by ¶ system of 22 degrees, and only un¶ come close to our equal intonation. ¶ the ruder scales Nos. 82 to 87 they cannot ¶ traced. In Java thev do not exist actnallv.

In China great difficulty was felt by the native musicians of the Health Exhibition of 1884 in respect to the Semitones. The Tones were variable, and in some cases there seemed to be a liking for the minor Tone as in tuning the Tamboura, No. 108. Iu Japan Semitones and Tones play a great part theoretically, but in the only practical cases I have been able to ¶ observe, Ncs. Ill and 112 both were very certain, and the Fourth was absent.
